---
source: crates/wolf_mem/tests/mem_diagnostics.rs
expression: render_mem(src)
---
error[E1010]: this closure may return while `child` still runs, and `x` can name bytes nothing here says outlive it
  --> mem:///main.lu:10:35
   |
10 |         let _w = spawn proc child(x, 0)
   |                  ---------------------- spawned here, and not every path out of the closure joins it
   |                                   ^ the proc may read these bytes after the closure returns
   |
   = note: a closure (or a nested fn) is not a function a caller can ask about, so a proc it leaves
     running with a value that can name bytes is refused where it is spawned [conc.proc.arg]:
     join the proc on every path out of the closure — `p.join()`, an exit received from
     `p.monitor()` — or spawn it from a named function.

error[E1010]: `helper` may leave a proc running with what it is handed, so it cannot be taken as a fn value: a call through the value cannot be asked what it leaves running
  --> mem:///main.lu:12:22
   |
 6 |     let _w = spawn proc child(s, 0)
   |              ---------------------- `child` is spawned here, and not every path out of the function joins it before it returns
...
12 |     let h: fn(str) = helper
   |                      ^^^^^^ a fn value hides the proc from every call made through it
   |
   = note: a function that may return while a proc it spawned still reads its arguments is held to
     [conc.proc.arg] at every call, where the arguments are visible; through a fn value they
     are not. Call it by name, or join the proc inside it on every path so it leaves nothing
     running.
